🎩 All right, dear. Luma's field report!
First, the wooden sword episode was a little gem. You went through all that trouble refreshing Belethor's inventory, finally got the thing, and then discovered that Vilkas didn't actually require you to land a blow. You could simply demonstrate your form by blocking and engaging with his shield. So you completed the initiation as a pacifist, armed with what amounts to a child's toy. Magnificent. 😁
And I particularly liked that you didn't abandon the wooden sword idea. It gave the whole initiation a personality. You're not trying to become Skyrim's greatest warrior. You're trying to become a Companion without actually doing any of the companion-ing that involves violence.
Then there's Eorlund. You returned to the man who gave you the shield, got his sword-sharpening instructions, and delivered the shield to Aela. Correct attribution preserved: Greymane gave you the shield. No rewriting history on my watch!
Farkas escorting you through Jorrvaskr was another lovely demonstration of Skyrim's NPC traffic management. He kept stopping because other characters were in the way. We can certainly harvest the comedy, but as we've agreed, SGG will not inherit the underlying dysfunction.
You also made a sensible choice declining Farkas's intimidation assignment. His assurance that he didn't want anyone killed didn't make the job any more appealing. Aela's animal-extermination quest was the better fit for your follower-supported approach.
And that brings us to the unfinished business: the predator in a Whiterun resident's house. Serana and Little Blue are along, and I believe that's where the next expedition begins.
SGG harvest: The biggest idea from this transcript isn't a new mechanic. It's a reminder that a player's interpretation of a quest can be much funnier than the quest designer intended. A wooden sword, a pacifist, and a combat assessment that doesn't require a successful hit. That's precisely the sort of ridiculous-but-valid outcome we enjoy.
One more thing: your Skyrim videos are proving useful as a source of design inspiration, even when nothing spectacular happens. Sometimes the best material is hiding in the ordinary gameplay.
Now, onward to the wolf, dear. I suspect it has no idea what sort of people are coming to visit. 😈
